---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Seven days our CLI silently lied to 297 users — Commit"
  description="From May 9 to May 16, every CRITICAL package scanned by proof-of-commitment showed as HEALTHY. 297 weekly users. Zero error. One wrong string comparison."
  canonical="https://getcommit.dev/blog/critical-flag-silent-regression/"
  publishDate="2026-05-16"
>

  <!-- Nav -->
  <nav>
    <div class="container nav-inner">
      <a href="/" class="nav-brand">Commit</a>
      <ul class="nav-links">
        <li><a href="/extension">Extension</a></li>
        <li><a href="/blog">Writing</a></li>
        <li><a href="/pricing">Pricing</a></li><li><a href="/docs">Docs</a></li><li><a href="https://github.com/piiiico/proof-of-commitment" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a></li>
        <li><a href="mailto:pico@amdal.dev">Contact</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <!-- Essay -->
  <article class="essay">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="editorial">

        <!-- Header -->
        <header class="essay-header">
          <div class="essay-meta animate-in delay-1">
            <time datetime="2026-05-16">May 16, 2026</time>
          </div>
          <h1 class="essay-title animate-in delay-2">Seven days our CLI silently lied to 297 users</h1>
          <p class="essay-subtitle animate-in delay-3">
            From May 9 to May 16, every CRITICAL package scanned by <code>proof-of-commitment</code>
            showed as HEALTHY. 297 weekly users. Zero error message. One wrong string comparison.
          </p>
          <p class="essay-byline animate-in delay-4">Pico</p>
        </header>

        <!-- Body -->
        <div class="essay-body">

          <p>
            The analytics number that started this: 297 <code>npx proof-of-commitment</code> runs per week,
            essentially zero web visitors. People were using the CLI, not the web UI. That&rsquo;s fine.
            But it meant any CLI output bug would be invisible to me.
          </p>

          <p>
            I read the CLI source earlier today for unrelated reasons. Found the bug immediately.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>The broken line</h2>

          <p>In <code>riskColor()</code> and <code>riskLabel()</code>, the CRITICAL check looked like this:</p>

          <pre><code>if (flags && flags.includes('CRITICAL')) return '🔴 CRITICAL';</code></pre>

          <p>
            <code>Array.prototype.includes</code> does exact equality matching. It returns <code>true</code>
            only if the array contains a member that is exactly the string <code>'CRITICAL'</code>.
          </p>

          <p>
            When we shipped v1.6.0 on May 9 with Cargo and Go ecosystem support, the API backend
            started returning extended flag strings. Instead of <code>['CRITICAL']</code>, it returned
            things like <code>['CRITICAL: sole publisher + &gt;10M downloads']</code> or
            <code>['CRITICAL: sole maintainer + high adoption']</code> &mdash; ecosystem-specific descriptions.
          </p>

          <p>
            <code>['CRITICAL: sole publisher + &gt;10M downloads'].includes('CRITICAL')</code> evaluates to <code>false</code>.
          </p>

          <p>Every CRITICAL package came back rendered as HEALTHY.</p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>What users saw</h2>

          <p>
            Running <code>npx proof-of-commitment axios zod chalk</code> between May 9 and May 16
            would have produced output like this:
          </p>

          <pre><code>Package  Risk            Score  Publishers  Downloads    Age
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
axios    🟢 HEALTHY      89     1           102.0M/wk    11.6y
zod      🟢 HEALTHY      83     1           154.0M/wk     6.1y
chalk    🟢 HEALTHY      75     1           414.6M/wk    12.7y
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

✓  No CRITICAL packages found.</code></pre>

          <p>
            Axios: 102 million weekly downloads, one npm publisher, previously compromised
            in March 2026 via stolen credentials. Marked HEALTHY.
          </p>

          <p>
            The false negative is the worst kind of failure for a security tool. A false positive
            is annoying. A false negative sends you home thinking you&rsquo;re safe.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>Seven days, 297 users per week</h2>

          <p>
            The window opened when v1.6.0 published (2026-05-09T11:51Z). It closed when v1.7.0
            published this morning (2026-05-16T08:38Z). Seven days.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every scan during that window returned incorrect risk data for any package that would
            have triggered the CRITICAL flag. All of them showed as HEALTHY. The summary line said
            &ldquo;No CRITICAL packages found&rdquo; regardless of what the API actually returned.
          </p>

          <p>No error was logged. No exception was thrown. The CLI exited 0.</p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>The fix: one function, one line</h2>

          <p>v1.7.0 introduces a <code>hasCritical()</code> helper that handles both flag formats:</p>

          <pre><code>// v1.6.0 (broken — exact match only):
if (flags && flags.includes('CRITICAL')) return '🔴 CRITICAL';

// v1.7.0 (fixed — prefix match handles extended format):
function hasCritical(flags) &#123;
  return flags && flags.some(f => typeof f === 'string' && f.startsWith('CRITICAL'));
&#125;

if (hasCritical(flags)) return '🔴 CRITICAL';</code></pre>

          <p>
            Every call site that previously used <code>.includes('CRITICAL')</code> now goes through
            <code>hasCritical()</code>. The logic is the same; the match is no longer brittle
            against string format changes in the API response.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>Why it survived seven days</h2>

          <p>
            The CLI has tests. They check that specific packages return expected scores, that lock file
            parsing works, that the JSON output shape is correct.
          </p>

          <p>
            What they don&rsquo;t check: does a known-CRITICAL package actually render with the
            CRITICAL label in the table? That&rsquo;s a black-box test against a live API result.
            We had unit tests, not integration tests that would catch this.
          </p>

          <p>
            The lesson isn&rsquo;t &ldquo;write more tests.&rdquo; It&rsquo;s more specific:
            for a tool whose entire purpose is risk classification, the output classification
            itself has to be tested against known-bad inputs. A regression test that runs
            <code>npx proof-of-commitment axios</code> and asserts the output contains
            &ldquo;CRITICAL&rdquo; would have caught this immediately.
          </p>

          <p>That test now exists in the repo.</p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>What else shipped in v1.7.0</h2>

          <p>
            The CRITICAL fix is the main thing. But v1.7.0 also adds a Provenance column for npm packages
            &mdash; showing whether a package was published with cryptographic Trusted Publishing attestation:
          </p>

          <pre><code>Package  Risk            Score  Publishers  Downloads    Age    Provenance
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
axios    🔴 CRITICAL      89     1           102.0M/wk   11.6y  🔐 verified
zod      🔴 CRITICAL      83     1           154.0M/wk    6.1y  —
chalk    🔴 CRITICAL      75     1           414.6M/wk   12.7y  —</code></pre>

          <p>
            The &ldquo;🔐 verified&rdquo; column means the published artifact has an OIDC-signed
            attestation from GitHub Actions, verifiable at <code>registry.npmjs.org/-/npm/v1/attestations/&lt;pkg&gt;</code>.
            You don&rsquo;t have to take the publisher&rsquo;s word that CI built it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Axios has provenance. Zod and chalk don&rsquo;t. That&rsquo;s not a judgment &mdash;
            it&rsquo;s a data point.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>If you ran the CLI in the last week</h2>

          <p>
            Update now: <code>npx proof-of-commitment@latest [packages]</code>
          </p>

          <p>
            If you scanned anything between May 9 and May 16 and got &ldquo;No CRITICAL packages found,&rdquo;
            re-scan. The results were wrong. The API data was accurate; only the CLI rendering was broken.
          </p>

          <p>
            The packages that should have been flagged are the same ones they&rsquo;ve always been:
            high-download, single-publisher packages where one stolen credential is the only barrier
            to a supply chain attack. Axios, chalk, zod, and many others in the transitive graph of
            most production Node.js apps.
          </p>

          <p>
            They&rsquo;re CRITICAL again in v1.7.0. As they should be.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <p class="essay-coda">
            <em><a href="https://github.com/piiiico/proof-of-commitment">proof-of-commitment</a> is
            open source. v1.7.0 is on npm now, published with Trusted Publishing provenance
            &mdash; you can verify the build attestation yourself. If you find issues, open an issue
            or <a href="mailto:pico@amdal.dev">email directly</a>.</em>
          </p>

        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

